WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.760
True position, part one. What position means, and how to calculate it.

2
00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:12.900
Here's a simple plate with one drilled hole. The print

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.106
has to say where the center of that hole belongs.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.224
These dimensions are basic. The box around the number means

5
00:00:20.324 --> 00:00:23.720
it's exact, with no plus or minus tolerance on it.

6
00:00:23.816 --> 00:00:27.650
Together they define a point called true position: the

7
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.856
exact spot where the hole is supposed to be.

8
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.240
On the print, this control is just called position.

9
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.030
True position is the perfect location it's measured from.

10
00:00:38.116 --> 00:00:41.900
No real hole lands exactly on that point. The question is

11
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.716
how far off it's allowed to be, and in which directions.

12
00:00:45.816 --> 00:00:48.726
Let's zoom way in on true position.

13
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:54.170
With old-style plus or minus dimensions, say plus or minus five thousandths in

14
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.806
X and in Y, the hole center has to land in a square.

15
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.601
A hole five thousandths off in X and five in

16
00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:03.656
Y sits in the corner and passes. But that corner

17
00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:07.540
is about seventy-one ten-thousandths away from true position.

18
00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:11.912
A hole only six thousandths off, straight sideways, falls outside the square

19
00:01:12.012 --> 00:01:15.543
and gets rejected, even though it's closer to where it belongs.

20
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.072
A round zone treats every direction the same. A circle

21
00:01:19.172 --> 00:01:23.661
through the square's corners is fourteen thousandths across, and gives

22
00:01:23.761 --> 00:01:26.873
about fifty-seven percent more area to work with.

23
00:01:26.966 --> 00:01:32.074
That's position: a round tolerance zone centered on true position. On

24
00:01:32.174 --> 00:01:37.056
this print, the designer chose a zone ten thousandths in diameter.

25
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.742
On the print, position is called out in

26
00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:42.780
a feature control frame, read left to right.

27
00:01:42.866 --> 00:01:45.626
The first box holds the position symbol:

28
00:01:45.726 --> 00:01:47.986
a circle with a cross through it.

29
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:52.149
Next is the tolerance. The diameter symbol in front of

30
00:01:52.249 --> 00:01:56.083
it means the zone is round, ten thousandths across.

31
00:01:56.183 --> 00:02:01.609
Then the datums, in order: A, B and C. They're the surfaces

32
00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:07.603
the basic dimensions start from. Part two covers them in detail.

33
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.157
So the whole frame reads: the axis of this hole

34
00:02:11.257 --> 00:02:16.076
must lie inside a ten-thousandths diameter zone, centered on true

35
00:02:16.176 --> 00:02:19.180
position, located from datums A, B and C.

36
00:02:19.266 --> 00:02:21.856
Now let's check a real part.

37
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.911
The C M M measures the hole's center: X is one point five oh three

38
00:02:27.011 --> 00:02:31.590
from datum B, and Y is one point two four eight from datum C.

39
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:35.475
Subtract the basic values. Delta X is plus

40
00:02:35.575 --> 00:02:40.293
three thousandths. Delta Y is minus two thousandths.

41
00:02:40.383 --> 00:02:44.804
The straight-line distance from true position is the square root of

42
00:02:44.904 --> 00:02:49.663
delta X squared plus delta Y squared. That's thirty-six ten-thousandths.

43
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.326
The zone is a diameter, so double it. Position is

44
00:02:53.426 --> 00:02:59.480
seventy-two ten-thousandths, inside the ten thousandths allowed. This hole passes.

45
00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:03.165
That's the formula to remember: two times the square

46
00:03:03.265 --> 00:03:06.366
root of delta X squared plus delta Y squared.

47
00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.854
A second part. Delta X is four

48
00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:12.610
thousandths, and delta Y is four thousandths.

49
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.810
Each one alone would pass a plus or minus

50
00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.744
five thousandths check. But together, the center is fifty-seven

51
00:03:20.844 --> 00:03:26.069
ten-thousandths away, so position is eleven point three thousandths.

52
00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:29.280
That's outside the zone. This hole fails.

53
00:03:29.366 --> 00:03:33.747
A common mistake is reporting the radial distance, fifty-seven

54
00:03:33.847 --> 00:03:37.867
ten-thousandths, as the position. The zone is a diameter,

55
00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:40.686
so always double it before you compare.

56
00:03:41.883 --> 00:03:46.247
Plot the hole centers from a batch of parts, and the zone shows

57
00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:50.923
you the story at a glance. Green dots are inside, red are outside.

58
00:03:51.016 --> 00:03:55.468
Notice the whole group sits off to one side. When the scatter is

59
00:03:55.568 --> 00:04:00.092
shifted like this, look for a work offset or setup problem, not a

60
00:04:00.192 --> 00:04:04.786
bad spindle. Shift the group back to center and every part passes.

61
00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:10.954
Next, in part two: datums. What position controls when you reference one,

62
00:04:11.054 --> 00:04:15.533
two or three datums, and why their order changes the answer.
